1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
In the precipitation video,
barium sulfate was the

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,320
safe one: it barely dissolves, so a barium

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,039
meal passes through the gut.

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:10,880
But barely isn't "not at all".

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,440
Some barium sulfate does dissolve.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:15,600
So how much, exactly?

7
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:17,520
You'll calculate it from one number in a

8
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:19,920
data table, and you'll see why a bigger

9
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:22,400
number in that table doesn't always mean a

10
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:23,680
more soluble salt.

11
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:28,220
Put excess barium sulfate
in water and stir.

12
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:30,980
A tiny amount dissolves, and the rest sits

13
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:31,900
on the bottom.

14
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:35,300
That's a saturated solution:
it holds as much

15
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:37,060
as it can at that temperature.

16
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,180
As you saw in the dissolving video, ions

17
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:42,580
keep leaving the solid and rejoining it at

18
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:43,460
equal rates.

19
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:47,140
So a saturated solution
is an equilibrium, with

20
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:51,060
an equation: solid barium
sulfate in equilibrium with

21
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:53,460
barium ions and sulfate ions.

22
00:00:54,667 --> 00:00:56,826
Every equilibrium has a K.

23
00:00:57,067 --> 00:00:58,506
Products go on top.

24
00:00:58,747 --> 00:01:01,066
The reactant is a solid, and solids are

25
00:01:01,187 --> 00:01:03,746
left out, so there's no bottom at all.

26
00:01:04,027 --> 00:01:06,826
K is just the barium
ion concentration times

27
00:01:06,907 --> 00:01:08,946
the sulfate ion concentration.

28
00:01:09,227 --> 00:01:12,026
It's a product of ion
concentrations, so it's

29
00:01:12,147 --> 00:01:15,186
called the solubility product, Ksp.

30
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:18,506
Like every K, it changes
only with temperature,

31
00:01:18,587 --> 00:01:20,426
and we write it without a unit.

32
00:01:20,747 --> 00:01:23,706
At 25 degrees, the data table gives barium

33
00:01:23,787 --> 00:01:27,546
sulfate a Ksp of 1.08 times ten to

34
00:01:27,627 --> 00:01:28,586
the minus ten.

35
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,520
If the formula has
a subscript, the equation

36
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:33,720
gets a coefficient.

37
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,040
Calcium fluoride, CaF₂, dissolves into

38
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:41,080
one calcium ion and two fluoride ions.

39
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:44,280
As in any K, the coefficient becomes a

40
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:49,160
power: Ksp is calcium,
times fluoride squared.

41
00:01:50,367 --> 00:01:53,086
Ksp isn't the solubility itself.

42
00:01:53,567 --> 00:01:56,606
The molar solubility, s, is the number of

43
00:01:56,687 --> 00:01:59,166
moles of solid that dissolve per litre, to

44
00:01:59,247 --> 00:02:00,766
make a saturated solution.

45
00:02:01,407 --> 00:02:04,046
To link the two, ask what one formula

46
00:02:04,127 --> 00:02:05,166
unit gives.

47
00:02:05,487 --> 00:02:08,606
Each barium sulfate that
dissolves gives one barium

48
00:02:08,687 --> 00:02:10,686
ion and one sulfate ion.

49
00:02:11,247 --> 00:02:14,286
So if s moles per litre dissolve, both

50
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:16,446
ion concentrations are s.

51
00:02:17,667 --> 00:02:19,266
Let's put it in an ICE

52
00:02:19,347 --> 00:02:23,106
table: I for initial, C for change, and

53
00:02:23,267 --> 00:02:24,546
E for equilibrium.

54
00:02:25,027 --> 00:02:28,466
In pure water, both ions start at zero.

55
00:02:28,547 --> 00:02:31,266
s dissolves, so the change is plus s

56
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:33,986
for each ion, and the equilibrium row is

57
00:02:34,067 --> 00:02:35,426
s and s.

58
00:02:35,467 --> 00:02:37,826
The solid gets no column in Ksp.

59
00:02:39,034 --> 00:02:43,273
Substitute: Ksp is s times s, which is

60
00:02:43,434 --> 00:02:47,273
s squared, and that equals 1.08 times ten

61
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:48,633
to the minus ten.

62
00:02:48,954 --> 00:02:51,593
Take the square root of both sides: s

63
00:02:51,674 --> 00:02:54,953
is 1.04 times ten to the minus five

64
00:02:55,114 --> 00:02:56,073
moles per litre.

65
00:02:56,634 --> 00:03:00,393
Check: 1.04 times ten to the minus five,

66
00:03:00,434 --> 00:03:03,993
squared, gives 1.08 times ten to the minus

67
00:03:04,154 --> 00:03:04,393
ten.

68
00:03:04,874 --> 00:03:08,313
Multiply by the molar mass, 233 grams per

69
00:03:08,394 --> 00:03:11,593
mole, and that's about 2.4 milligrams in a

70
00:03:11,674 --> 00:03:12,393
whole litre.

71
00:03:12,954 --> 00:03:14,713
That's what barely means.

72
00:03:15,934 --> 00:03:19,293
Now calcium fluoride, with a Ksp of 3.45

73
00:03:19,454 --> 00:03:22,733
times ten to the minus eleven.

74
00:03:22,854 --> 00:03:25,693
Each formula unit gives
one calcium ion and

75
00:03:25,774 --> 00:03:27,413
two fluoride ions.

76
00:03:27,694 --> 00:03:31,533
So if s dissolves, calcium rises by s,

77
00:03:31,614 --> 00:03:34,413
but fluoride rises by two s.

78
00:03:34,454 --> 00:03:37,773
The equilibrium row is s and two s.

79
00:03:37,854 --> 00:03:41,773
Substitute: Ksp is s, times two s, all

80
00:03:41,934 --> 00:03:43,053
squared.

81
00:03:43,134 --> 00:03:45,293
Two s all squared is two s times

82
00:03:45,374 --> 00:03:48,093
two s, which is four s squared, not

83
00:03:48,174 --> 00:03:49,453
two s squared.

84
00:03:49,534 --> 00:03:52,253
So Ksp is four s cubed.

85
00:03:53,467 --> 00:03:57,386
Four s cubed equals 3.45 times ten to

86
00:03:57,427 --> 00:03:58,426
the minus eleven.

87
00:03:58,907 --> 00:04:02,186
Divide both sides by four: s cubed is

88
00:04:02,267 --> 00:04:05,546
8.63 times ten to the minus twelve.

89
00:04:05,787 --> 00:04:08,666
Then take the cube root: s is 2.05

90
00:04:08,747 --> 00:04:11,866
times ten to the minus four moles per

91
00:04:11,987 --> 00:04:12,506
litre.

92
00:04:12,747 --> 00:04:17,226
The fluoride concentration
is two s: 4.10 times

93
00:04:17,307 --> 00:04:18,666
ten to the minus four.

94
00:04:19,867 --> 00:04:23,226
The classic slip
is writing s for fluoride,

95
00:04:23,387 --> 00:04:24,426
instead of two s.

96
00:04:24,987 --> 00:04:28,826
Then Ksp looks like s cubed, and s

97
00:04:28,907 --> 00:04:31,546
comes out as 3.26 times ten to the

98
00:04:31,627 --> 00:04:35,306
minus four: about 1.6 times too big.

99
00:04:35,627 --> 00:04:37,706
The two comes from the formula, so it

100
00:04:37,787 --> 00:04:40,506
goes inside the bracket,
before you square.

101
00:04:41,734 --> 00:04:43,573
Now compare the two salts.

102
00:04:43,814 --> 00:04:47,333
Barium sulfate has
the bigger Ksp, but calcium

103
00:04:47,414 --> 00:04:51,213
fluoride's molar solubility
is about twenty times bigger.

104
00:04:51,494 --> 00:04:52,213
How?

105
00:04:52,534 --> 00:04:56,213
Barium sulfate's Ksp
is s squared, and calcium

106
00:04:56,294 --> 00:04:58,613
fluoride's is four s cubed.

107
00:04:58,854 --> 00:05:02,053
Different formulas give
different powers, so their Ksp

108
00:05:02,134 --> 00:05:04,453
values aren't on the same scale.

109
00:05:04,694 --> 00:05:07,893
Compare Ksp values
directly only for the same

110
00:05:08,054 --> 00:05:11,733
formula type, like barium
sulfate against silver chloride.

111
00:05:12,214 --> 00:05:14,693
Otherwise, calculate s for each.

112
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:17,820
It also works backwards.

113
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,420
A saturated magnesium
hydroxide solution is found to

114
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:24,860
contain 1.12 times ten to the minus four

115
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:28,660
moles per litre of dissolved
magnesium hydroxide.

116
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:31,180
This time we have a measured value, so

117
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:34,780
it goes into the equilibrium
row first: magnesium

118
00:05:34,860 --> 00:05:38,620
ions, 1.12 times ten to the minus four.

119
00:05:38,860 --> 00:05:42,060
The change is equilibrium
minus initial, the same

120
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:42,700
amount.

121
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:47,260
Each formula unit gives
two hydroxide ions, so

122
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:51,580
hydroxide rises by twice
that: 2.24 times ten

123
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:53,180
to the minus four.

124
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:58,460
Ksp is magnesium times
hydroxide squared: 5.62 times

125
00:05:58,540 --> 00:05:59,820
ten to the minus twelve.

126
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:02,860
Forget to double
the hydroxide, and your Ksp

127
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:05,020
comes out four times too small.

128
00:06:06,234 --> 00:06:07,433
So here's the habit.

129
00:06:07,914 --> 00:06:10,873
Each ion is its count times s.

130
00:06:11,114 --> 00:06:12,873
Then raise it to that count.

131
00:06:13,434 --> 00:06:15,673
One to one gives s squared.

132
00:06:15,754 --> 00:06:18,473
One to two, either way round, gives four

133
00:06:18,554 --> 00:06:19,513
s cubed.

134
00:06:20,734 --> 00:06:21,373
Your turn.

135
00:06:22,014 --> 00:06:25,693
Lead iodide has a Ksp of 9.8 times

136
00:06:25,774 --> 00:06:28,413
ten to the minus nine at 25 degrees.

137
00:06:28,974 --> 00:06:31,533
Calculate its molar solubility in water.

138
00:06:32,014 --> 00:06:33,293
Pause here to answer.

139
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:46,660
Each lead iodide gives one lead ion and

140
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:51,220
two iodide ions, so Ksp is s times

141
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:54,340
two s squared: four s cubed.

142
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:56,660
Divide both sides by four, take the cube

143
00:06:56,740 --> 00:07:00,100
root, and s is 1.35 times ten to

144
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:02,020
the minus three moles per litre.

145
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:04,900
If you got 2.14 times ten to the

146
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:07,700
minus three, you used s instead of two

147
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:08,980
s for iodide.

148
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:10,840
Last one.

149
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,000
A saturated solution of silver carbonate,

150
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:19,880
Ag₂CO₃, contains 0.0354 grams

151
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:20,600
per litre.

152
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:25,400
Its molar mass is 275.8 grams per mole.

153
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:27,640
Calculate Ksp.

154
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:29,480
Pause here to answer.

155
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:45,020
First, convert to moles: 0.0354 divided

156
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:49,740
by 275.8 is 1.28 times ten to

157
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:51,900
the minus four moles per litre.

158
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:55,580
That measured value goes
in the equilibrium row:

159
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:58,860
carbonate is s, and silver is two s.

160
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:03,180
Ksp is two s squared, times s: 8.46

161
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:05,980
times ten to the minus twelve.

162
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:08,940
Keep the unrounded s in your calculator.

163
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:13,420
If you put 0.0354 straight in as s,

164
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:15,420
you skipped the conversion to moles.

165
00:08:16,634 --> 00:08:20,233
So, for any Ksp question:
write the dissolving

166
00:08:20,314 --> 00:08:23,273
equation, and each ion as its count times

167
00:08:23,354 --> 00:08:23,753
s.

168
00:08:24,314 --> 00:08:27,753
Given Ksp, solve for s: for salts like

169
00:08:27,834 --> 00:08:30,473
these, a square or cube root.

170
00:08:30,554 --> 00:08:32,873
Given a measured solubility, put it in the

171
00:08:32,994 --> 00:08:36,153
equilibrium row first, then multiply out.

172
00:08:36,234 --> 00:08:39,273
And compare Ksp directly only for the same

173
00:08:39,354 --> 00:08:40,713
formula type.

174
00:08:40,794 --> 00:08:43,353
Next: two solutions are mixed.

175
00:08:43,434 --> 00:08:45,433
Will a precipitate actually form?

176
00:08:46,634 --> 00:08:48,073
Here are the key notes.

177
00:08:48,314 --> 00:08:50,233
Pause the video if you need more time

178
